00:01:38.974 --> 00:01:44.157
Alright, cleaned up here a
little bit in the boat bathroom.

00:01:44.157 --> 00:01:47.068
Fully in the Cannes mode now

00:01:47.068 --> 00:01:50.068
and super fired up 
to do our first

00:01:50.068 --> 00:01:52.121
event here at the VaynerX boat.

00:01:52.121 --> 00:01:54.323
VaynerX is our 
holding company that holds

00:01:54.323 --> 00:01:56.742
PureWow and VaynerMedia.

00:01:56.742 --> 00:01:57.492
We're here in Cannes.

00:01:57.492 --> 00:01:58.827
We've got a 
bunch of activations.

00:01:58.827 --> 00:02:00.704
Ryan Harwood, CEO 
of PureWow is here.

00:02:00.704 --> 00:02:01.538
- Excited to be here.

00:02:01.538 --> 00:02:03.206
- Which means 
everything will be okay.

00:02:03.206 --> 00:02:04.624
(Ryan laughs)

00:02:04.624 --> 00:02:07.969
Row of bunch 
of boats to the left

00:02:07.969 --> 00:02:09.649
and to the right.

00:02:09.649 --> 00:02:12.424
Some people 
massively overdressed.

00:02:12.424 --> 00:02:15.725
Right there, those characters.

00:02:19.303 --> 00:02:20.432
Going to do some panels now.

00:02:20.432 --> 00:02:21.725
You wore a sports jacket?

00:02:21.725 --> 00:02:23.586
- I was like this is not good.

00:02:23.586 --> 00:02:24.925
Not gonna work this time.

00:02:24.925 --> 00:02:28.511
(upbeat music)

00:02:59.454 --> 00:03:01.014
- [Gary] Tommy B!

00:03:01.014 --> 00:03:03.781
What up, baby?
How are you?

00:03:05.633 --> 00:03:06.324
How are you?
- I'm very well.

00:03:06.324 --> 00:03:07.189
Nice to meet you.
- [Gary] What's your name?

00:03:07.189 --> 00:03:08.328
- Joelle.
- [Gary] Joelle.

00:03:08.328 --> 00:03:10.002
- Will. (inaudible)

00:03:10.002 --> 00:03:11.662
- How are things?
- Very well. Very well.

00:03:11.662 --> 00:03:12.487
- Good.

00:03:12.487 --> 00:03:13.699
- [Tom] Way better 
dressed than I am dude.

00:03:13.699 --> 00:03:16.699
Don't know what I was thinking. 
I'm wearing board shorts.

00:03:16.699 --> 00:03:18.994
- Dude, the rave 
is about to start, Tom.

00:03:18.994 --> 00:03:20.445
You're good. 
You're good, brother.

00:03:20.445 --> 00:03:23.140
I actually think 
it's one macro which is

00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:25.250
this is a binary conversation.

00:03:25.250 --> 00:03:27.043
Either an organization cares

00:03:27.043 --> 00:03:29.504
about it's employees 
or it doesn't.

00:03:29.504 --> 00:03:32.757
And by the way I actually
think the way to hack this is to

00:03:32.757 --> 00:03:36.469
actually make this a 
CFO issue not an HR issue.

00:03:36.469 --> 00:03:38.722
I think when people 
actually understand at

00:03:38.722 --> 00:03:41.141
the highest levels that it's...

00:03:41.141 --> 00:03:45.187
Listen, I'm a good guy and I
like people but like the reason

00:03:45.187 --> 00:03:48.523
I'm obsessive about 
retention is it creates speed.

00:03:48.523 --> 00:03:52.152
People know each other, 
cadence and speed wins.

00:03:52.152 --> 00:03:53.570
You know, business is sports.

00:03:53.570 --> 00:03:57.887
Speed wins and so for me, when
we announced Claude as our

00:03:57.887 --> 00:04:00.629
Chief Heart Officer and I started
doing more stuff on HR and just

00:04:00.629 --> 00:04:03.107
gave a big speech in 
San Francisco around HR

00:04:03.107 --> 00:04:06.361
when I talk about people 
I try to make it more of

00:04:06.361 --> 00:04:10.584
a CFO conversation because 
if you don't it's fluffy,

00:04:10.584 --> 00:04:12.452
it's emotional, 
it's nice to have.

00:04:12.452 --> 00:04:14.106
If you make it financial,

00:04:14.106 --> 00:04:15.565
everybody starts 
paying attention.

00:04:15.565 --> 00:04:18.610
So to me, you 
level it up to why.

00:04:18.610 --> 00:04:22.248
Maybe you give somebody a 
raise that you don't want to or

00:04:22.248 --> 00:04:24.315
you give them tickets to a 
Cardinals game or

00:04:24.315 --> 00:04:27.601
you buy them some sneakers 
that they've been liking on

00:04:27.601 --> 00:04:28.769
Pinterest or Instagram.

00:04:28.769 --> 00:04:32.683
It shows that you care 
and that leads to retention.

00:04:32.683 --> 00:04:34.816
The biggest reason I spend so
much time one on one with all

00:04:34.816 --> 00:04:36.067
the faces I see here.

00:04:36.067 --> 00:04:38.820
People that are still here,
people that used to work for me

00:04:38.820 --> 00:04:43.033
is 'cause that caring 
ROI plays out in a macro.

00:04:43.033 --> 00:04:43.904
Shelly spoke to it.

00:04:43.904 --> 00:04:46.895
I mean, you will never find
somebody who's won that ever

00:04:46.895 --> 00:04:49.210
talks about short term return.

00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:52.528
Everybody who's winning is
thinking long term because

00:04:52.528 --> 00:04:55.577
the ironic thing is business 
life and life in general,

00:04:55.577 --> 00:04:58.673
hopefully is long if nothing 
crazy happens is a marathon.

00:04:58.673 --> 00:05:01.635
Yet, everybody's running 
around like it's a sprint.

00:05:01.635 --> 00:05:06.181
Trying to make something happen
in 30 or 90 or 180 day window.

00:05:07.161 --> 00:05:10.829
I think the hack is can you
get into the ear of the CFO?

00:05:10.829 --> 00:05:13.814
Can she or he 
really realize, holy shit,

00:05:13.814 --> 00:05:15.839
this actually matters 
more than you think.

00:05:15.839 --> 00:05:18.890
And I think one of the, I invest
in a company called HiQ which is

00:05:18.890 --> 00:05:21.636
real big data 
analyzation of all this.

00:05:21.636 --> 00:05:23.540
The cost of replacing engineers

00:05:23.540 --> 00:05:26.027
in Silicon Valley 
is so ridiculous.

00:05:26.027 --> 00:05:28.476
Even for me, with the 
people that I interact with,

00:05:28.476 --> 00:05:31.437
even if they're mid-tier
managers the emotional cost of

00:05:31.437 --> 00:05:33.815
replacement is pretty extreme

00:05:33.815 --> 00:05:35.984
and so that's 
where I would go with it.

00:05:35.984 --> 00:05:39.195
(upbeat music)

00:05:44.802 --> 00:05:46.947
I think I speak about 
regret so much 'cause

00:05:46.947 --> 00:05:49.099
I'm so fearful of it.

00:05:49.099 --> 00:05:52.642
I love life so much that when
I'm 84 and I know I don't have

00:05:52.642 --> 00:05:57.495
30 years left, I'm gonna be sad.

00:05:57.495 --> 00:06:01.358
The reason I tell 
you this is you're young.

00:06:01.358 --> 00:06:04.114
I don't know what you're
expenses are or debt is, right?

00:06:04.114 --> 00:06:05.365
But the bottom line is,

00:06:05.365 --> 00:06:08.052
it's always easier 
now than it is tomorrow.

00:06:08.052 --> 00:06:09.665
- [Joelle] Yeah.
- [Gary] It just is.

00:06:09.665 --> 00:06:11.847
- [Joelle] I told you, 
I was shaking your hand--

00:06:11.847 --> 00:06:13.085
- Done! Done!

00:06:13.085 --> 00:06:14.207
- [Joelle] I'm gonna implement
and I know what you're like

00:06:14.207 --> 00:06:16.595
and I know you're 
gonna hold me to it.

00:06:16.595 --> 00:06:18.704
- What's even crazier 
is I would fuckin' hire you

00:06:18.704 --> 00:06:20.559
if your startup fails.
I would flat out do it.

00:06:20.559 --> 00:06:22.904
Just for the story, 
we would run this video.

00:06:22.904 --> 00:06:24.348
You know?
- [Joelle] Alright, I love that.

00:06:24.348 --> 00:06:25.412
- Good luck to you.
- [Joelle] Thanks very much.

00:06:25.412 --> 00:06:26.259
- Thanks for stopping by.

00:06:26.259 --> 00:06:27.586
Cheers, mate. Stay well.

00:06:27.586 --> 00:06:30.000
Hi. I'm glad you came.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:31.894
Really good to see you.
(crosstalk)

00:06:31.894 --> 00:06:32.480
How are things?

00:06:32.480 --> 00:06:34.066
- [Woman] Nadine Kaiser.
- Nadine, how are you?

00:06:34.066 --> 00:06:35.744
- [Woman] Nadine's actually 
one of your neighbors.

00:06:35.744 --> 00:06:37.321
She's (censored).

00:06:37.321 --> 00:06:40.459
(upbeat music)

00:06:45.230 --> 00:06:46.481
- [DRock] How'd the panel go?

00:06:46.481 --> 00:06:48.667
- Panel went super well. 
Shelly's a beast.

00:06:48.667 --> 00:06:51.636
Straight winner. 
Important issue.

00:06:51.636 --> 00:06:56.447
You know, diversity and
inclusion is not about checking

00:06:56.447 --> 00:06:58.272
a box of being 
politically correct.

00:06:58.272 --> 00:07:00.670
It's the smart 
fuckin' strategy to win.

00:07:00.670 --> 00:07:02.088
(indistinct chatter)

00:07:05.455 --> 00:07:07.145
I'll be right back up.

00:07:09.160 --> 00:07:10.959
Hey, hey. First one down.

00:07:10.959 --> 00:07:13.730
(upbeat music)

00:07:18.148 --> 00:07:21.651
And Dog is in the building.

00:07:21.651 --> 00:07:23.298
Looking good, Dog.

00:07:25.838 --> 00:07:27.528
- [Dennis] Dennis
Ossipov-Grodsky,

00:07:27.528 --> 00:07:29.882
universally known as Dog.

00:07:29.882 --> 00:07:33.434
(laughs) Edit that.

00:07:33.434 --> 00:07:35.873
- [DRock] What's the most
valuable thing you've learned

00:07:35.873 --> 00:07:38.815
while working with Gary?

00:07:38.815 --> 00:07:43.270
- To be honest with you, 
I think he embodies change.

00:07:44.940 --> 00:07:47.364
Change has been a 
beaten up word, right?

00:07:47.364 --> 00:07:50.059
Everybody talks about change.

00:07:50.059 --> 00:07:53.852
I've been to companies 
that did talk change

00:07:53.852 --> 00:07:56.654
but change took forever

00:07:56.654 --> 00:08:01.729
and market course
corrected them and with Gary

00:08:01.729 --> 00:08:04.216
I think he embodies change

00:08:04.216 --> 00:08:07.106
and he imposes that mentality

00:08:07.106 --> 00:08:10.195
organically, by the way, on
the rest of the organization.

00:08:10.195 --> 00:08:13.547
And I have not seen an 
organization that was more

00:08:13.547 --> 00:08:17.116
nimble and flexible 
in accepting change.

00:08:17.116 --> 00:08:21.454
Pivoting, adjusting, adjusting
to the market conditions,

00:08:21.454 --> 00:08:24.579
adjusting to his vision,
right, because he has incredible

00:08:24.579 --> 00:08:28.152
intuition and 
proving it right or wrong.

00:08:28.152 --> 00:08:32.511
So I think it's also important
to not to be precious about your

00:08:32.511 --> 00:08:34.941
change because change comes.

00:08:34.941 --> 00:08:36.226
Sometimes it's the right change,

00:08:36.226 --> 00:08:38.268
sometimes it's semi-right change,

00:08:38.268 --> 00:08:40.206
sometimes it's outright wrong.

00:08:40.206 --> 00:08:44.143
So it's very important for
organization to be able to go

00:08:44.143 --> 00:08:48.020
into that change, test it
out and say two months later,

00:08:48.020 --> 00:08:48.870
"You know what?

00:08:48.870 --> 00:08:50.461
"It was a mistake, 
we're pulling out.

00:08:50.461 --> 00:08:51.417
"We're changing it."

00:08:51.417 --> 00:08:52.719
So change.

00:08:52.719 --> 00:08:55.426
- I think, and it's such a
cliche and it's something that

00:08:55.426 --> 00:08:57.717
he says all the time,

00:08:57.717 --> 00:09:00.137
but I think it's a 
marathon and not a sprint.

00:09:00.137 --> 00:09:03.345
I think this week at Cannes is
actually probably one of the

00:09:03.345 --> 00:09:05.845
best representations of that.

00:09:05.845 --> 00:09:07.669
You know when 
you think about it,

00:09:07.669 --> 00:09:09.153
there's a lot of different
people that we're gonna be

00:09:09.153 --> 00:09:12.645
meeting from a lot of different
companies and a lot of people

00:09:12.645 --> 00:09:14.416
here are thinking 
more transactional.

00:09:14.416 --> 00:09:16.209
What's the short 
term opportunity?

00:09:16.209 --> 00:09:17.377
How do I sell them today?

00:09:17.377 --> 00:09:20.380
How do I close the deal?

00:09:20.380 --> 00:09:22.297
Where the way that I'm thinking
about it and it's really what

00:09:22.297 --> 00:09:25.071
I've learned from Gary is how
do I actually build a genuine

00:09:25.071 --> 00:09:29.181
relationship with this person
with the idea that we might not

00:09:29.181 --> 00:09:31.391
do anything 
together for two, three,

00:09:31.391 --> 00:09:32.919
four, ten years.

00:09:32.919 --> 00:09:34.728
But ultimately, I might want to

00:09:34.728 --> 00:09:36.607
recruit that person 
ten years from now.

00:09:36.607 --> 00:09:39.920
Or I might want to do a 
deal ten years down the line.

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:42.986
Or they're gonna go 
talk to their friend and say,

00:09:42.986 --> 00:09:44.571
"Hey, I just met 
with the VaynerMedia team.

00:09:44.571 --> 00:09:45.292
"They're really, really smart.

00:09:45.292 --> 00:09:47.977
"You should be thinking 
about what they're doing."

00:09:47.977 --> 00:09:50.994
And so it's really just coming
at it with a long game mentality

00:09:50.994 --> 00:09:53.955
versus how do I make 
the most money or

00:09:53.955 --> 00:09:56.875
close the deal 
in the short term.

00:09:56.875 --> 00:09:59.925
Which is a total mind shift for
most people because they're in

00:09:59.925 --> 00:10:02.964
it for the short game 
versus kinda the

00:10:02.964 --> 00:10:05.093
30, 40, 60 year game plan.

00:10:05.093 --> 00:10:09.200
- At the party, 
Maverick doing his thing.

00:10:09.200 --> 00:10:10.137
Bunch of big wigs.

00:10:10.137 --> 00:10:11.325
Kelly McCarthy.

00:10:11.325 --> 00:10:13.590
She helped me build 
VaynerMedia in the early days.

00:10:13.590 --> 00:10:15.977
Mav Carter, one of 
the great executives,

00:10:15.977 --> 00:10:18.653
LeBron's boy.

00:10:18.653 --> 00:10:22.072
Hustlin' at this event, 
making shit happen.

00:10:22.072 --> 00:10:23.167
Alright, it's getting 
late in the night.

